from pathlib import Path
import json
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3];out=root/'results/full-scaling';m=out/'macos'
main=json.loads((m/'options-summary.json').read_text());size=json.loads((m/'size-control-summary.json').read_text());native=json.loads((m/'matched-native-summary.json').read_text())
assert all(not x['clock_flags'] and x['all_fingerprints_match'] for x in [main,size,native])
d=lambda x:{(r['backend'],r['encoding'],r['mode']):r for r in x['results']};a=d(main);b=d(size);c=d(native)
def sec(data,backend,encoding,mode):return data[backend,encoding,mode]['seconds_median']
lines=['# macOS: answer to Timo’s performance question','','Encoding and build configuration both matter on this M4 Max. The complete replacement can improve substantially without importing the whole experimental adapter. The comparisons below keep installed-package results separate from matched native builds.','','All three phases use the same 1,126,185,409-byte ASCII-body input and a UTF-8-declared copy with identical file size/index offsets, one reader, cached input, OpenMP/BLAS=1, and five randomized fresh processes per cell: 140 option runs, 60 size-control runs and 80 matched-native runs. All **280 fingerprints agree**, libraries stay unchanged within each phase, and there are no elapsed/process/CPU consistency flags. Opening times exclude the following 2,048 queries; process peak RSS includes initialization and those queries. Absolute times vary between phases; compare within a phase.','','## Build flags: more than O2 versus O3','','The installed Homebrew formula’s `configure` command does not establish the effective compiler flags. Homebrew’s [Clang optimization default is `Os`](https://github.com/Homebrew/brew/blob/7b8d758/Library/Homebrew/extend/ENV/super.rb#L96), including the brew revision named in this bottle’s install receipt; its compiler wrapper can inject build settings. Thus `configure` versus CMake Release alone is insufficient evidence for the earlier “libxml2 O2 / Xerces O3” assumption. Exact historical bottle invocations were not recovered.','','A matched unpatched libxml2 2.15.4 source build at `-Os` reproduces the bottle’s Latin-1 times within 0.1%; changing that build to `-O2` reduces streaming by about 14% and opening by 23%. The earlier matched `-O2`/`-O3` comparison changes little and has overlapping ranges. This strongly supports a size-versus-speed optimization contribution, not a large O2-versus-O3 effect.','','| libxml2 build, Latin-1 | Stream s | Default open s |','|---|---:|---:|']
for backend,label in [('full-homebrew','Homebrew bottle'),('full-os','Same source, local -Os'),('full-o2','Same source, local -O2')]:lines.append(f"| {label} | {sec(b,backend,'ISO-8859-1','stream'):.3f} | {sec(b,backend,'ISO-8859-1','indexed-metadata'):.3f} |")
lines+=['','![Build controls](figures/macos-build-controls.png)','','## Source-level options and a fair Xerces control','','Two private experiments preserve the parser’s encoding/error machinery: an eight-byte ASCII copy path inside libxml2’s Latin-1 converter, with the original non-ASCII/capacity fallback; and an OpenMS shortcut that skips the ASCII scan only for binary text that a metadata-only read discards. General non-ASCII fallback and derived-handler dispatch remain intact. An earlier broad shortcut failed a direct handler-contract test and was discarded. No experimental patch changes the published production implementation or the canonical Linux runs.','','In the 140-run option phase, the combination reduces Latin-1 streaming/opening times by **23.1%/47.6%** versus the current Homebrew-backed replacement. Its RSS remains about 52 MiB for streaming and 104 MiB for open plus queries, versus about 57/118 MiB for the Xerces package reference. The [ablation graph](figures/macos-options.png) shows compiler, converter and ignored-text changes separately.','','Optimizing Xerces too changes the ranking, so the matched control is essential:','','| Native -O3 comparison | Declaration | Stream s | Default open s |','|---|---|---:|---:|']
for backend,label in [('xerces-native-o3','Xerces 3.3.0'),('full-o3','Complete libxml2 2.15.4'),('discard-ascii-o3','Private combination')]:
 for enc in ['ISO-8859-1','UTF-8']:lines.append(f"| {label} | {enc} | {sec(c,backend,enc,'stream'):.3f} | {sec(c,backend,enc,'indexed-metadata'):.3f} |")
lines+=['','Under matched native `-O3`, the private combination still trails Xerces by **13.7% for Latin-1 streaming and 16.9% for Latin-1 opening**. On UTF-8, the unpatched native builds are near parity (libxml2 −0.3%/−2.3%), and the combination opens 13.7% faster than native Xerces. The apparent win against the Xerces bottle is therefore not a universal library ranking. Building libxml2 alone improves end-user latency, but does not normalize the comparison: Xerces also benefits, and a large relative Latin-1 gap persists in matched builds. The declaration control is stronger evidence for the encoding contribution than a claim that build flags alone explain the macOS/Linux ratio difference. macOS Xerces uses its CoreFoundation transcoder; Linux uses GNU iconv, another reason not to attribute every platform difference solely to the operating system.','','## Practical choices','','1. **Build libxml2 for speed at -O2 or -O3.** This is the first option to try for Homebrew-backed macOS setups: a private, pinned parser build with the required SAX1/PUSH/SCHEMAS/THREAD features and encoding converters. OpenMS itself was already built at -O3. Test the resulting package rather than assuming a formula or Release label determines parser code generation.','2. **Emit honest UTF-8 for new files where the serializer supports it.** This removes Latin-1 conversion altogether. The declaration-only experiment is valid because this generated body is entirely ASCII; arbitrary existing Latin-1 files require actual transcoding and regenerated indexes/checksums, not a blind header edit.','3. **Consider the two isolated patches for upstreaming.** The native converter keeps BOM/declaration/enforced-encoding handling inside libxml2; it avoids a separate adapter that sniffs declarations and suppresses encoding handling. The ignored-binary shortcut is format-specific and smaller than a shared parser rewrite. Both remain prototypes requiring broader platform/sanitizer validation before adoption.','4. **Profile remaining name/attribute costs before a larger adapter change.** Timo’s SAX2/UTF-8-attribute branch was inspected but not benchmarked here. A global SAX2 switch would revisit the legacy xQuest/idXML namespace behavior already covered by regression tests; it is not an automatic safe speed switch.','','## Validation and limits','','Each local libxml2 build passed all eight native CTest suites. The converter passed 51,472 byte/boundary/random oracle cases plus 65 exact in-place ASCII cases, matching unpatched output/status/consumption digests. The combined candidate passed 12 focused OpenMS regressions and 24 additional same-length payload controls: non-ASCII Latin-1/UTF-8 text can still be ignored by metadata-only reads, while invalid UTF-8 and undefined entities still reject; streaming outcomes/digests also match. Kimi and Claude Opus 5.5 reviewed the isolated patches with no blocking introduced defects; Codex checked the actual caller/fallback context. Partial overlapping converter buffers are unsupported, and skip-predicate duplication remains a maintenance consideration.','','The native Xerces control passed the OpenMS benchmark correctness suite and 79/80 upstream CTest cases. Its DOMTest relative-baseURI assertions also fail identically when the same unchanged test binary loads the installed Xerces bottle; a no-space working-directory control did not resolve them. This shared upstream-test failure is retained, not silently relabeled as a passing suite. A legacy curl framework accidentally selected during initial configuration was replaced with the system SDK library before any matched timing run.','','Raw records, hashes, build flags, tests, review receipts, and reproduction scripts accompany the report. No function-level Time Profiler trace was collected; the evidence isolates encoding/build/source changes but does not assign an exact sample share to a converter function.']
(out/'MACOS_OPTIONS.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n');print('Wrote macOS options report')
